  "transcript": "Deep inside a trained network, one unit keeps firing. Show it a dog, and it lights up. So, have you found a dog neuron? What would that claim actually mean? The usual evidence is a gallery. The nine inputs that excite this unit the most. Every one of them a dog. Case closed? But that gallery was picked after the fact. It is just the top of a ranking. Look at the rest of the data, and the same unit answers to plenty of other things. A handful of winning pictures cannot tell you what a unit ignores. Selectivity is not a claim about one picture. It is a claim about the whole distribution of this unit's responses. So, run the whole dataset through, record this unit's activation for every input, then split those numbers by the label of the input. What should Selective even look like here? One class parked high, every other class down low. That is a selective unit. All the classes piled on top of each other, and the unit answers to everything, which tells you nothing about class. To turn that picture into a number, collapse each class to its mean response. The biggest of those class means is the unit's favorite class. Call it mu max. Average the means of all the remaining classes. That is the rest. Now compare the two. Take the gap between the favorite and the rest and divide that gap by their sum. That ratio is the class selectivity index. Read the two ends. One means only the favorite class moves this unit at all. The rest sit at zero. And zero means every class means the same thing to it. No class preference at all. Why divide at all? Because a raw gap can be faked. Turn the volume up on this unit, scale every response by the same factor, and the raw gap grows. While the shape of the distribution is untouched, the ratio does not budge. One warning: a high index does not mean the network relies on this unit. Delete the most selective unit and accuracy can barely flinch. Delete a unit with a low index and the damage can be worse. And the index only ever sees the labels you handed it. A unit that looks mixed across your classes can be sharply tuned to something you never measured. A texture, a color, a curve. Plenty of units answer to several unrelated things at once. So a selective unit is one whose response distribution separates its favorite class from all the rest, measured over the whole dataset and normalized so loudness cannot fake it. It describes what a unit responds to. It does not prove the network reads it.",
